175 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 96 0 R
/E (Formal education systems across the East and Horn of Africa were built around a static assumption, that children stay in one place long enough to attend a fixed school on a fixed calendar. Yet for pastoral communities, this assumption has never held, and key informant interviews for this study confirm that the mismatch between mobile livelihoods and static education systems remains one of the most consistent and least resolved findings in the entire dataset. Fixed school locations, long travel distances, rigid academic calendars and household labour demands combine to produce low enrolment and high dropout, a pattern that falls hardest on girls.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
176 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 96 0 R
/E (This brief argues that policy responses so far has been insufficient not because good alternative models do not exist, but because they have rarely been implemented, funded or scaled with the seriousness given to static education systems. Decades of research on mobile schools, alternative basic education and distance learning for pastoralist children show what works and what does not. The evidence gap is not technical, it is one of political will, sustained investment, and willingness to treat mobility as a permanent design parameter rather than a temporary problem to be solved or wished away through sedentarization.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
