216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 99 0 R
/E (Kenya's experience with mobile schools illustrates both the promise and the fragility of these alternatives. A qualitative case study of mobile schools in Turkana County found the model persistently undermined by inadequate teacher numbers and weak integration pathways into the formal education system, despite mobile schooling's conceptual fit with nomadic pastoralist life \(Ngugi, 2016\). A more recent study of mobile schools in Samburu County, using a transformative innovation policy lens, found that mobile schooling can meaningfully improve enrolment, retention and completion when properly resourced, but that it remains a niche innovation whose system-level impact depends on sustained investment and integration with health, security and other sectors rather than treatment as a standalone education intervention \(Transformation Innovative Policy Consortium, 2020\).  )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
217 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 99 0 R
/E (Country-specific research reinforces the gendered dimension of this exclusion identified in this data. A study of girl-child education in Baringo County, Kenya, applying an access framework of accessibility, availability, acceptability, adaptability and affordability, found that all five dimensions independently constrained pastoralist girls' school participation, with early marriage, adherence to pastoralist cultural norms and poverty compounding the purely logistical barriers created by mobility itself \(Wakhisi et al, 2025\). This finding directly supports our conclusion that mobility-related barriers and gender-related barriers operate simultaneously rather than as separate problems requiring separate solutions. There are intersectional dynamics to be considered in such scenarios.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
