/P 102 0 R
/T (2. The scale of exclusion is severe, particularly in Somalia)
/E (2. The scale of exclusion is severe, particularly in Somalia)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 102 0 R
/E (Nomadic pastoralists make up an estimated two-thirds of Somalia's population, yet only around 22 percent of pastoralist children access any formal education \(Dyer, 2016\). This is not a marginal enrolment gap, and it represents systemic exclusion of a majority-population livelihood group from the formal education system entirely.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 102 0 R
/T (3. Girls face compounding barriers beyond mobility itself)
/E (3. Girls face compounding barriers beyond mobility itself)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
244 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 102 0 R
/E (Beyond the general mismatch between mobility and static schooling, girls face additional and compounding barriers due to household labour expectations, early marriage, and, in Somalia specifically, female genital mutilation affecting an estimated 98 percent of girls, frequently followed by school withdrawal and early child marriages \(Jama & Barre, 2019\). For instance, a Baringo County study applying an access-and-affordability framework found that all five access dimensions constrain pastoralist girls' schooling simultaneously, with cultural norms compounding rather than simply adding to logistical barriers \(Wakhisi, 2025\). )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
245 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 102 0 R
/T (4. Climate shocks directly intensify education exclusion)
/E (4. Climate shocks directly intensify education exclusion)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
246 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 102 0 R
/E (Drought and climate-related economic pressure are documented drivers of early marriage and school withdrawal and are not indirect or theoretical risks, but an active, measured pattern. For example, UNICEF reporting found that the number of children at risk of dropping out across Ethiopia, Kenya and Somalia tripled within three months during a recent drought period, and that child marriage more than doubled within a year in the worst-affected regions of Ethiopia, as families turned to early marriage as a negative coping mechanism for economic hardship \(UNICEF USA, 2024\).)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
