/P 103 0 R
/T (5. Effective alternative models exist but remain inconsistently scaled)
/E (5. Effective alternative models exist but remain inconsistently scaled)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
255 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (Mobile schools, flexible calendars, community-based teachers, boarding facilities, radio and offline learning, vocational education and targeted girls' support were all identified by respondents as viable alternatives. The evidence base confirms this. For instance, a Samburu County study found mobile schooling can meaningfully improve enrolment and retention when properly resourced, though a separate Turkana County study found the same model persistently undermined by inadequate teacher numbers and weak integration into the formal system , thereby illustrating that the model works in principle but frequently fails in practice due to insufficient sustained investment \(Ngugi, 2016\).)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
256 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 103 0 R
/T (6. Indigenous knowledge remains excluded from formal curricula)
/E (6. Indigenous knowledge remains excluded from formal curricula)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
258 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (Women's knowledge of weather patterns, livestock management, seed preservation, food processing, water management, medicinal plants and conflict resolution was repeatedly identified as a resilience resource, yet formal policy, research and education systems continue to treat this knowledge as informal or outdated rather than validating and integrating it alongside scientific and technical content.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (This is consistent with the broader global critique that policy attention to nomadic education has focused disproportionately on physical access i.e. getting a school or mobile unit within reach, while neglecting whether the content of learning itself reflects nomadic livelihoods and cultural values \(Dyer, 2016\).)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
