268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 104 0 R
/T (The evidence in this brief supports a specific conclusion, that the barrier to aligning education with pastoral mobility is not a lack of known solutions. Mobile schools, flexible calendars, distance and radio-based learning, and community-led programmes addressing the social drivers of dropout have all been piloted, studied and, in some cases, proven effective over several decades of research across multiple countries and contexts. )
/E (The evidence in this brief supports a specific conclusion, that the barrier to aligning education with pastoral mobility is not a lack of known solutions. Mobile schools, flexible calendars, distance and radio-based learning, and community-led programmes addressing the social drivers of dropout have all been piloted, studied and, in some cases, proven effective over several decades of research across multiple countries and contexts. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 104 0 R
/T (What has been missing is sustained investment at the scale required to move these models from pilot projects to system-level infrastructure as well as the willingness to treat pastoral mobility as a permanent feature of the communities being served, rather than a logistical obstacle to be minimized through sedentarization or fixed geography. )
/E (What has been missing is sustained investment at the scale required to move these models from pilot projects to system-level infrastructure as well as the willingness to treat pastoral mobility as a permanent feature of the communities being served, rather than a logistical obstacle to be minimized through sedentarization or fixed geography. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
270 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 104 0 R
/T (The Somalia data point is relevant here; a 78 percent exclusion rate for pastoralist children is not the result of an unsolved technical problem, but of a persistent gap between documented best practice and actual investment.)
/E (The Somalia data point is relevant here; a 78 percent exclusion rate for pastoralist children is not the result of an unsolved technical problem, but of a persistent gap between documented best practice and actual investment.)
/Pg 21 0 R
