<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (The recommendations below are derived directly from the key informant interview findings presented in this brief and are further supported by the regional and global literature reviewed above. They translate this brief's core argument; that effective alternatives to static schooling are well documented but under-invested.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
278 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 105 0 R
/T (1. Scaling Proven Alternative Education Models)
/E (1. Scaling Proven Alternative Education Models)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
279 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 594 0 R
/E (Move mobile schools from pilot to system-level infrastructure - scale mobile schools, flexible calendars and community-based teaching with sustained multi-year funding, learning directly from relevant examples, such as Kenya's Samburu County experience, where the model improved enrolment and retention when properly resourced, and its Turkana County experience, where the same model was undermined specifically by inadequate teacher numbers.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
280 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 595 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
281 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 596 0 R
/E (Invest specifically in teacher recruitment and retention for mobile settings - address the single most recurring failure point identified across multiple case studies, notably that of inadequate teacher numbers, through targeted incentives \(e.g. hardship allowances, mobile-teacher career pathways\) rather than treating mobile schooling as a curriculum design problem alone.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 105 0 R
/T (2. Addressing the Social Drivers of Girls' Dropout)
/E (2. Addressing the Social Drivers of Girls' Dropout)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 597 0 R
/E (Fund community-led alternatives to harmful practices directly - invest in and replicate AMREF Health Africa's Community-Led Alternative Rite of Passage \(CL-ARP\) programme model from Kajiado County, which kept 98 percent of participating girls in school and prevented FGM/C and early marriage in a comparable share of cases over a three-year follow-up period, rather than funding school-access interventions alone \(Kawai et al., 2025\).)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
