1190 0 obj
<<
/Title (Approximately two-thirds of Somalia's population is pastoralist, and only around one-fifth of pastoralist children in Somalia receive any formal education, if at all. This gap driven by fixed school locations, cultural practices, and the structural incompatibility between mobile livelihoods and static schooling models \(Jama & Barre, 2019\). Again, this gap is not a marginal access problem but is a systemic exclusion of a substantial share of the school-age population from any formal educational pathway.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1186 0 R
/Next 1191 0 R
>>
endobj
1191 0 obj
<<
/Title (This brief builds on evidence generated specifically for this study through key informant interviews with 20 pastoralist women-led, humanitarian, climate-resilience, policy and peacebuilding organizations across Kenya, Somalia, Tanzania and South Africa. That evidence shows education exclusion is not gender neutral. Girls face compounding barriers beyond the general mismatch between mobility and static schooling due to household labour expectations and early marriages among other factors. In Somalia specifically, near-universal female genital mutilation, which is frequently followed by withdrawal from school and early child marriages. Climate shocks intensify all of these pressures simultaneously, as recent UNICEF reporting on drought-driven child marriage across Ethiopia, Kenya and Somalia demonstrates.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1186 0 R
/Prev 1190 0 R
>>
endobj
