1204 0 obj
<<
/Title (In Somalia specifically, the scale of exclusion is severe as nomadic pastoralists constitute roughly two-thirds of the population, yet only around 22 percent of pastoralist children access formal education, a gap compounded by the practice of female genital mutilation, which affects an estimated 98 percent of Somali girls and is frequently followed by school withdrawal due to resulting health complications and early child marriage \(Jama & Barre, 2019\). This is corroborated by UNICEF's regional reporting, which found that the number of children at risk of dropping out of school across Ethiopia, Kenya and Somalia tripled within three months during a recent drought period; from 1.1 million to an estimated 3.3 million, placing adolescent girls at substantially elevated risk of early marriage as a negative household coping mechanism \(Wakhisi et al, 2025; UNICEF USA, 2024\).)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1200 0 R
/Next 1209 0 R
>>
endobj
1205 0 obj
<<
/Title (Finally, a broader review of evolving global approaches to educating nomadic children cautions that policy attention has often overemphasized physical access logistics,  getting a school building or mobile unit near enough to reach, albeit at the expense of considering whether the content and structure of learning itself fits nomadic livelihoods and cultural values, a critique that applies directly to this study's parallel finding that indigenous ecological knowledge remains excluded from formal curricula even where physical access to schooling improves \(Dyer, 2016\).)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1209 0 R
/Next 1201 0 R
>>
endobj
