1209 0 obj
<<
/Title (Evidence on effective interventions is more encouraging. A longitudinal study of AMREF Health Africa's Community-Led Alternative Rite of Passage \(CL-ARP\) programme in Kajiado County, Kenya, designed to prevent female genital mutilation/cutting and child marriage while keeping girls in school, followed 2,647 girls over three years and found that 98 percent remained free of FGM/C, 99 percent avoided child, early or forced marriage, and 98 percent remained enrolled in school, although girls who experienced threats or violence remained more likely to experience all three negative outcomes despite programme participation \(Kawai et al., 2025\). This finding is directly relevant to this brief's recommendations, that culturally grounded, community-led alternatives to harmful practices can produce durable educational gains when they address the social drivers of dropout directly, rather than focusing on school access alone.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1204 0 R
/Next 1205 0 R
>>
endobj
1210 0 obj
<<
/Title (Respondents consistently described formal education as insufficiently adapted to nomadic and semi-nomadic communities. Fixed school locations, long travel distances, insecurity, rigid academic calendars, seasonal migration and household labour demands combine to produce low enrolment and dropout, with girls affected disproportionately. This finding was reported by key informants across all four study countries i.e.  Kenya, Somalia, Tanzania and South Africa, thus indicating a shared structural gap rather than an isolated national issue.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1206 0 R
/Next 1211 0 R
>>
endobj
